{"step_index":9,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:25:07Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences: Master Examination Guide\r\nComprehensive 32-Question Master Bank (05-Mark Long-Form Model Answers)\r\nQUESTIONS 01 – 32 5-MARK EXAMINATION FORMAT SYSTEMIC PATHOLOGY • CRITICAL CARE • DERMATOLOGY • OB-GYN\r\nPART I: RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY & PULMONARY MEDICINE (Q01 – Q07)\r\nQ 01 Acute Bronchitis\r\nDefinition: An acute, self-limiting inflammation of the tracheobronchial mucosa presenting characteristically with persistent cough with or\r\nwithout sputum production.\r\nEtiology:\r\nViral (>90%): Influenza A/B, Parainfluenza, RSV, Rhinovirus, Adenovirus, Coronavirus.\r\nBacterial (<10%):Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, Bordetella pertussis.\r\nNon-Infectious: Chemical fumes, dusts, tobacco smoke, air pollutants.\r\nClinical Features: Persistent cough (initially dry/hacking, later mucoid or purulent, lasting 1–3 weeks); retrosternal burning soreness on\r\ncoughing; low-grade fever, malaise, myalgias; scattered expiratory wheezes/rhonchi and coarse crackles that clear upon coughing.\r\nInvestigations: Primarily clinical diagnosis. Chest X-ray (PA view) is usually normal; indicated to exclude pneumonia if tachypnea,\r\ntachycardia, high fever, or consolidation signs are present. Sputum PCR only for suspected B. pertussis.\r\nComplications: Secondary bronchopneumonia, acute asthma/COPD exacerbation, chronic post-infectious bronchial\r\nhyperresponsiveness (>4 weeks).\r\nManagement: Supportive hydration and steam inhalation; Paracetamol/Ibuprofen for chest soreness and fever; Dextromethorphan for\r\ndisruptive dry cough; inhaled Salbutamol for bronchospasm/wheeze. Routine antibiotics are not recommended unless elderly with\r\ncomorbidities or confirmed pertussis (Azithromycin).\r\nQ 02 Bronchial Pneumonia (Bronchopneumonia)\r\nDefinition: Acute suppurative inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma centered around terminal and respiratory bronchioles,\r\npresenting with patchy, multifocal consolidation involving multiple pulmonary lobes.\r\nCauses: Bacterial: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphyl\n<truncated 48333 bytes>\nveries,\r\npostmenopausal estrogen deficiency, obesity, chronic cough).\r\nUrge Incontinence (UUI / Overactive Bladder, ~20%): Involuntary leakage preceded by an intense, sudden, uncontrollable urge to\r\nvoid. Caused by detrusor overactivity (idiopathic, stroke, Parkinson's, MS, UTI, bladder stones).\r\nMixed Incontinence (~30%): Features of both stress and urge incontinence.\r\nOverflow: Involuntary dribbling from a chronically overfilled, distended bladder due to bladder outlet obstruction (pelvic prolapse) or\r\nhypotonic detrusor (diabetic neuropathy).\r\nFunctional: Inability to reach toilet due to severe physical or cognitive disability (dementia, severe arthritis).\r\nClinical Features: Leakage on strain/cough (SUI); urinary urgency, daytime frequency (>8 times), and nocturia (UUI); continuous\r\ndribbling (overflow); perineal skin dermatitis, secondary UTIs, anxiety, and social withdrawal.\r\nManagement:\r\nConservative & Behavioral (First-line): Lifestyle: weight loss, fluid moderation, avoid caffeine/alcohol; Pelvic Floor Muscle\r\nTraining (Kegel exercises): at least 3 sets of 8–12 maximal contractions daily for ≥3 months (first-line for SUI); Bladder training\r\n(timed voiding) for UUI.\r\nPharmacological: For UUI: Antimuscarinics (Oxybutynin, Tolterodine, Solifenacin) or β3-agonist (Mirabegron); Local topical vaginal\r\nestrogen for postmenopausal women; SUI: Duloxetine (SNRI).\r\nInterventional & Surgical: Intravesical OnabotulinumtoxinA injections or sacral neuromodulation for refractory UUI; Mid-Urethral\r\nSling (MUS: TVT / TOT) synthetic mesh placement (gold standard for refractory SUI); Burch colposuspension; Periurethral bulking\r\nagents.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 12 of 12\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-06T18:55:07+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
