{"step_index":11,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-08T05:12:28Z","content":"**GTU Mid-Sem Master Cheat Sheet: Tier 1 & Easy Wins (UPDATED)**\nThis document contains exactly what you must write to get full marks for the highest-probability topics, including all necessary diagrams, advantages, and limitations required for 7-mark GTU questions.\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER 1: FOUNDRY TECHNOLOGY**\n\n**1. Six Applications of Casting (3 Marks):** Easy Win (Appeared in actual mid-sem). Write these exact 6 points: 1. Automotive engine blocks and cylinder heads. 2. Machine tool beds and frames (due to damping capacity). 3. Turbine blades (aerospace). 4. Railway wheels and tracks. 5. Pump housings and valve bodies. 6. Agricultural machinery components.\n\n**2. Pattern Allowances - Theory (7 Marks):** Tier 1. What to write for full marks: • Definition: Intentional dimensional modifications made to a pattern to compensate for metal shrinkage, machining, and casting defects. • 1. Shrinkage Allowance: Metals shrink when cooling from liquid to solid. Pattern is made larger. (Values: CI = 10mm/m, Steel = 20mm/m, Al = 13mm/m). • 2. Machining/Finish Allowance: Extra material added to surfaces that require machining later. (Usually 1.5 to 6 mm). • 3. Draft Allowance: Taper applied to vertical faces of the pattern for easy withdrawal without damaging the mould. (1° to 3° external, 2° to 5° internal). • 4. Shake/Rapping Allowance: When a pattern is shaken to remove it, the mould cavity enlarges. Pattern is made slightly smaller to compensate. (Negative allowance). • 5. Distortion/Camber Allowance: Applied to U or V-shaped castings that tend to warp during cooling. Pattern is bent in the opposite direction. • Diagram Required (Crucial for 7M): Draw a 2D cross-section of a solid block. Show a dotted line outside for \"Machining Allowance\" and angled vertical sides for \"Draft Allowance\".\n\n**3. Pattern Allowance - Numerical (7 Marks):** Tier 1 (From actual mid-sem). How to score full marks: 1. Read dimensions: Identify L, W, H of the final casting. 2. Add Shrinkage: Add the % or mm/m shrinkage to each dimension.\n<truncated 5697 bytes>\nsqrt{R \\cdot \\Delta t}$. 3. True Strain: $\\epsilon = \\ln(\\frac{h_0}{h_1})$. 4. Average Flow Stress ($\\bar{Y}_f$): If given directly, use it. (If given as $K\\epsilon^n$, then $\\bar{Y}_f = \\frac{K\\epsilon^n}{n+1}$). 5. Roll Force (F): $F = \\bar{Y}_f \\cdot w \\cdot L_c$ (where w = width of plate). 6. Torque (T): $T = F \\cdot a$ (where $a$ is the moment arm. Usually $a = 0.5 \\cdot L_c$ for hot rolling). For ONE roll. 7. Total Power (P): $P = 2 \\cdot \\frac{2\\pi N T}{60}$ (Multiply by 2 because there are two rolls).\n\n**13. Extrusion: Forward vs Backward (7 Marks):** Tier 1. What to write for full marks: • Forward (Direct) Extrusion: Billet is placed in a chamber. Ram pushes the billet. The metal flows in the same direction as the ram movement. Sketch: Show ram pushing right, metal exiting right. Advantage: Simpler tooling. Disadvantage: High friction between billet and chamber walls. High force required. High scrap (butt). • Backward (Indirect) Extrusion: Die is mounted on a hollow ram. Ram pushes into the billet. Metal flows in the opposite direction to the ram movement through the hollow ram. Sketch: Show ram pushing right, metal exiting left (backwards) through the hollow ram. Advantage: Zero wall friction. 25-30% less force required than forward extrusion. Disadvantage: Mechanically complex tooling, hollow ram limits the maximum load that can be applied.\n\n**14. Wire vs Tube Drawing (4-7 Marks):** Tier 1. • Wire Drawing: Pulling a solid metal rod through a converging die to reduce its diameter. Force is applied as tension at the exit. • Tube Drawing: Reducing the diameter and wall thickness of a hollow tube. • Key Difference for Full Marks: Tube drawing requires a mandrel inside the tube to support the internal walls and control the final internal diameter. Wire drawing has no internal support. • Diagrams to Draw: 1. Wire drawing die (solid metal passing through). 2. Tube drawing setup showing the die AND the Mandrel. • Types of Mandrels (Mention for 7M): Fixed mandrel, floating mandrel, moving mandrel.","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Adapting to User Feedback**\n\nI'm now grappling with the need to adapt to user feedback, which seems to contradict some of my established guidelines. Specifically, the request to adjust the format directly challenges the previous rigid rules. I need to balance following instructions with responding to evolving user preferences. I'm prioritizing flexibility while still striving for efficiency.\n\n\n**Refining Output Structure**\n\nI'm now working on restructuring my outputs to find a balance between compactness and readability. The user's feedback suggests a need to merge the strictly formatted style with a more concise presentation. I'm considering using standard punctuation, strategic line breaks for topic separation, and keeping blocks concise. The original format included no punctuation, no line breaks, main topics using `---`, subtopics bolded, and lists.\n\n\n**Analyzing Format Adjustments**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the need to combine the user's formatting suggestions while staying concise, especially as the user wants the ability to understand things more clearly. The feedback wants adjustments to the strict formatting rules, but also wants compactness, and wants it \"mixed up\" to be easily understood. It looks like the original format that had no punctuation and no line breaks is a readability problem. My plan is to restore basic punct